    "content": "Middle class in America is generally defined as households earning between two-thirds and double the national median income. Based on 2024–2026 data, this typically translates to an annual household income of roughly $39,000 to $117,000, with national estimates varying slightly to around $56,600–$169,800 for a family of three. Key Middle Class Income Metrics: National Range: Households earning roughly $56,600 to $169,800 annually (for a 3-person household) are considered middle income, according to a Pew Research Center analysis of 2022 data. Pew Research Center 2026 Updated Data: A SmartAsset 2026 study suggests a broader, lower range of approximately $38,993 to $116,980 for a median household, depending on location. SmartAsset 2026 study Location Variation: Middle-class income heavily depends on cost-of-living. In high-cost states like California or Hawaii, the range is significantly higher, often requiring over $63,000 to reach the lower end of the middle class. Household Size: The definitions are often adjusted, with larger families needing higher incomes to maintain the same standard of living. Characteristics of the American Middle Class: Size: About 51% of American households fall into this category, a figure that has shrunk over the past five decades. Assets & Expenses: Middle-class families often face increasing pressure from costs of housing, childcare, and education. Employment: Typical jobs include white-collar positions, specialized skilled trades, and managerial roles. Because income varies significantly by location, a middle-class salary in a low-cost area may be considered low-income in a high-cost city.",
